WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.020
Split was one of the coolest movie-going experiences
I’ve ever had.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.259
When that ending hit, I just had chills.

00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:09.110
The energy in the theater was amazing during
that final scene.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:13.299
But it wasn’t until I went back and rewatched
it later that I truly realized how brilliant

00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:14.299
it was.

00:00:14.299 --> 00:00:16.360
There were hints about the ending all throughout
the movie.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.220
If you want to hear about all of them, then
stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.340
I’d recommend watching my Things You Missed
in Unbreakable first, since I will be referencing

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.220
it.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.060
[INTRO]

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.990
Both Split and Unbreakable open with a birthday
scene.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:36.039
In Unbreakable, it was the birth of Elijah
Price, and in Split, Claire Benoit celebrates

00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:37.039
her birthday.

00:00:37.039 --> 00:00:40.969
After the party, her dad offers a ride to
her classmate, Casey Cooke, but he never quite

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:42.079
makes it into the car.

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:46.359
Last week, I described how David Dunn and
Elijah Price are color coded, with David being

00:00:46.359 --> 00:00:49.159
associated with green and Elijah being purple.

00:00:49.159 --> 00:00:51.180
The Horde has their own color too -- yellow.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:55.809
It can be seen in Dennis’s handkerchief,
Hedwig's jacket, the flowers placed around

00:00:55.809 --> 00:00:59.109
the holding room and at the train station,
the lighting and wall colors throughout the

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:03.000
area below maintenance as well as the tiles
above ground at the zoo.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.440
In the poster for Glass, the colors of each
character are made clear.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.230
There are 24 sets of titles in the opening
and closing credits of Split, one for each

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.160
of the 24 identities of The Horde.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.660
The opening credits also have letters connected
by a vertical line in a way that makes it

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:20.160
look like two entries on a timeline, just
as this is the second entry in this series.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.710
The accompanying music by West Dylan Thordson
sounds like distorted animal cries.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:28.360
Take a listen.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:36.580
[CLIP]

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.970
So the girls are kidnapped and put in this
holding room.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.490
Claire and Marcia have different ideas about
how to get out than Casey, and the room is

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.180
vertically split, like the title of the movie,
by this power line to show the divide.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.690
Vertical divisions of the frame are also a
technique used a lot in Alfred Hitchcock’s

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:51.760
horror classic, Psycho.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.420
M Night Shyamalan, the director of Split,
lists Hitchcock as a huge influence, he even

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.160
released a 10 minute video on his appreciation
of Hitchcock’s Strangers on a Train.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.140
There are other Psycho references in Split,
but we’ll get to them when we get to them.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.720
Casey tells Marcia to pee herself when Dennis
takes her out of the room -- hoping to prevent

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.379
Dennis from forcing her to do something she
doesn’t want to do.

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:14.660
The fact that Dennis is a germaphobe works
in her favor, but that was really just luck.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.410
Around this time, one of the other identities,
Barry, reaches out to their psychologist,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:19.410
Dr. Fletcher.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.579
She’s got several other emails on the screen.

00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:25.569
Just as the art department of Unbreakable
had cameos in the newspaper, the crew of Split

00:02:25.569 --> 00:02:27.569
makes cameos in Dr. Fletcher’s email.

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:30.550
Charles S Rowe is credited as a miscellaneous
crew member.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:35.090
John B West, Dominic Catanzarite and John
Rusk were part of the production department.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:40.120
Mitch Campbell, Lucas Andrei, Jennifer Wessner,
Ed Mendez and Bob Lowery all worked visual

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.120
effects.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.470
Skip Lievsay, Bryan Baker, Adam Leach and
Matthew Shapiro were part of the post production

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:46.470
team.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.110
And Lisa Liberati is James McAvoy’s girlfriend.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.980
One of the emails mentions Cezanne, the French
painter, we’ll come back to that later.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.409
I love all these movies with just disturbing
pictures in the background.

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:59.579
Anyway, Barry comes to see Dr. Fletcher, but
it’s not really Barry.

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:03.269
Dr. Fletcher asks if he’s looking for something,
but he’s actually putting the room in order,

00:03:03.269 --> 00:03:05.790
because it’s really Dennis, the guy with
OCD.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.569
Imagine having a disorder within your disorder.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:09.939
Maybe Mr. Glass can relate.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.080
“I have something called osteogenesis imperfecta.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.440
It’s a genetic disorder.”

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.100
My name is Dr. Ellie Staple, and I'm a psychiatrist.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.140
I specialize in those individuals

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.440
Who believe they are superheroes.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.520
Anyway, the supposed Barry talks about making
a jacket hand printed with newspaper headlines.

00:03:31.529 --> 00:03:35.730
“That’s supposed to be like a tailored
jacket, but I’m gonna handprint it with

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:36.730
newspaper headlines.”

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.459
I know someone who happens to have a collection
of newspaper headlines.

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:43.879
Also in Dr. Fletcher’s office there’s
a picture on the wall of what appears to be

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:48.329
a woman looking behind a shower curtain. this
is one of those Psycho references I was talking

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:49.180
about.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:55.159
[Clip]

00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:57.599
After Dennis leaves, Dr. Fletcher goes through
her patient files.

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:01.319
The only one that’s legible is this one
on a Mr. Douglass Latreille.

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:06.120
As far as I can tell, the name is not relevant,
but the dates on the file range from July

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.040
2000 to November 2000.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.920
November 2000 is when Unbreakable was released,
and most of the movie is set within these

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.829
dates, for example the encounter with The
Orange Man in Unbreakable takes place just

00:04:15.829 --> 00:04:17.860
before October 23rd 2000.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.440
She also has a bin labeled ‘Universal Offices’.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.540
You can probably guess who produced this movie.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.180
Dr. Fletcher visits an art gallery to view
this Cezanne painting, probably the one she

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:30.560
received the email about.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.050
This piece is called the Bathers.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:36.330
Would it be too much of a stretch to relate
this to David Dunn’s swimming pool accident

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:37.960
or fear of water?

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:41.591
If you’re answer is yes, that is too much
of a stretch, then fine, but there’s no

00:04:41.591 --> 00:04:46.639
denying this line from Dr. Fletcher’s associate
Joe, was an intentional clue.

00:04:46.639 --> 00:04:51.400
“You treat them like they’re supernaturally
gifted, like they have powers or something.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:52.830
Karen, these are patients.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.620
They have been through trauma.”

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.410
Whoa, that’s a good one.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.930
I’m curious if any of you picked up on that
the first time through, and at what point

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:01.889
did you actually realize this was a sequel.

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:05.740
I tried to look for some more clues in Dr.
Fletcher’s notes on the various identities,

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.129
but I couldn’t really make out anything.

00:05:07.129 --> 00:05:10.680
I don’t know if this is supposed to be a
joke on that whole trope about doctors having

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.800
bad handwriting, but I can’t read any of
it, so let’s go back to Casey and the girls.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.270
Casey awakes from a flashback and we see her
point of view which is sideways.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:29.009
When I talked about Unbreakable, I examined
the right-side-up vs upside-down shots in

00:05:29.009 --> 00:05:33.510
that movie, so maybe these sideways shots
in Split are a bit of an in-between.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:36.980
This is also one of the first time we get
a good peek out of the holding room and there’s

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:41.379
a container of Barnum Animal Crackers, one
of the earliest clues towards their location

00:05:41.379 --> 00:05:42.810
being the Philadelphia Zoo.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:46.860
“It took forever to get this place safe
without the nosy-bodys who work here finding

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:47.860
out.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:49.009
You can’t get out of here!”

00:05:49.009 --> 00:05:53.120
Back at Dr. Fletcher’s office, we’ve got
our M Night cameo in the form of this guy

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:56.849
who helps Dr. Fletcher review security camera
footage of Dennis deliberately trying to prove

00:05:56.849 --> 00:05:59.210
that he’s Barry by walking through the trash.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:02.639
When Patricia brings Casey and Marcia into
the kitchen, you can briefly see that they

00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:06.490
all have different cereal preferences, which
I found to be an interesting detail.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:09.911
One of the things I talked about in Unbreakable
is how we always see Elijah through a glass

00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:12.970
reflection to represent the fragile nature
of his disorder.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:16.580
We see Elijah though the a reflection each
time he is introduced at a new age.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.819
In Split, we see Dennis in the reflection
of this certificate just before he first introduces

00:06:20.819 --> 00:06:22.289
himself to Dr. Fletcher.

00:06:22.289 --> 00:06:26.460
There’s another reflection scene later on,
which I’ll come back to.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:30.690
But in this conversation, we find out that
the 24th identity, The Beast, resides away

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:35.000
from the other 23 identities in a train yard,
because Kevin’s dad left on a train when

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.229
he was young, presumably, that was Eastrail
177.

00:06:38.229 --> 00:06:42.120
Casey convinces Hedwig to show her his room,
which, in another clue about them being under

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.849
the zoo, is filled with animal posters and
toys.

00:06:44.849 --> 00:06:49.129
Additionally the song that he famously dances
to in his room is called “Frogbass” by

00:06:49.129 --> 00:06:51.800
the artist currently known as Snails.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:07:07.280
[CLIP]

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.840
And this is actually a pretty good drawing
of Reshiram if that’s what it’s supposed

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:11.870
to be.

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:14.889
Hedwig shows Casey the walkie talkie that
he stole from Dennis, and Casey uses it to

00:07:14.889 --> 00:07:18.259
try to call help, but the man on the other
end thinks that it’s just his friend pulling

00:07:18.259 --> 00:07:21.210
his leg and assures him he still has the orange
headphones.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:24.000
When Casey eventually does get out at the
end of the movie, we do see him listening

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.319
to the orange headphones before he calls the
police.

00:07:26.319 --> 00:07:30.550
But, before any of that, there’s a very
important flashback where young Casey holds

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:35.450
her abusive Uncle at gunpoint, in a scene
that’s very, very reminiscent of Joseph

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:37.289
holding David at gunpoint in Unbreakable.

00:07:37.289 --> 00:07:38.280
Roll it.

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.080
"This isn't funny"

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:43.980
"Joseph, what the hell are you doing?"

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:45.740
“Is it loaded?”

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:47.560
“Joseph did you load that gun?”

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:50.260
“Casey”
 “Joseph.”

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:52.120
“I’m your Uncle.”

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:53.260
“Now I am your father…”

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.659
“Stop it Casey.”

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:55.659
“Joseph!”

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.220
“Put that gun down.”

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:00.220
“...and I’m telling you to put
that ******* gun down right now!”

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:02.440
“I’m getting upset.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.800
I’m about to get very angry!”

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.780
“You are about to be in big
trouble!”

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:11.220
"TWO!"

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.900
“You could have killed me.

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.800
These scenes take place at almost the exact
same point in both movies.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.450
Maybe one day I’ll do a side by side comparison,
but that sounds like another video.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:44.550
Dr. Fletcher’s concern leads her to try
to talk to The Horde at his home, so she pull

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:45.550
up in a taxi.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:49.780
Remember how the Channel 4 news logo in Unbreakable
was very reminiscent of the Fantastic Four

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:50.780
logo?

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:54.839
Well the letter T in this taxi cab service
is also a reference to the Teen Titans logo.

00:08:54.839 --> 00:08:59.170
The pieces are all coming together as Dennis
visits the train station to transform into

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:03.550
The Beast, and it’s an Amtrak train just
like Eastrail 177.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:07.440
Casey explores The Horde’s computer, and
this is where we see video diarys of each

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:08.440
member.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.060
One of them is named Norma, which completes
the trifecta of Psycho references, this one

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:13.910
referencing Norma Bates.

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:17.970
At some point, Dr. Fletcher realizes she probably
won’t make it out alive, but she leaves

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:19.620
a note for the captive girls to find.

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:22.800
The note is written on a receipt from “Hagenbaugh
Laboratories”.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:24.160
Which is not a thing.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:28.060
BUT, Emily Hagenbaugh is the author of this
superhero book.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:31.970
So you know how the rest goes, Casey has her
confrontation with the Beast, the Beast sees

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:35.560
that she too has gone through abuse and lets
her live.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:38.740
Someone finds her and escorts her from the
below maintenance area and the orange headphones

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:40.100
guy calls the police.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:44.010
One of the most disturbing things to me, is
that even after all that Casey has been through,

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.810
she still doesn’t want to go back with her
Uncle at the end.

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:48.430
That’s how bad he is.

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:51.610
But there’s one more loose end that hasn’t
been tied up.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:52.610
On my end.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:56.170
I mentioned the Unbreakable reference where
we see The Horde in the reflection in Dr.

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:58.930
Fletcher’s office, just like those Elijah
Price scenes from the first movie.

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:03.230
The final scene with The Horde goes all in
on this, and includes the panning back and

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:06.410
forth camera movement, as seen in many of
the scenes of Unbreakable.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:11.300
All the while James Newton Howard’ s Unbreakable
soundtrack slowly transitions in as we cut

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.850
to the credits and the iconic in-credit scene
and the reveal of David Dunn.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:18.910
I’ve got goosebumps just thinking about
it.

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:22.880
Now the stage is set for Glass and I could
not be more excited.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.420
So join me next week as I breakdown everything
you might have missed in the conclusion of

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:29.730
the Eastrail 177 trilogy.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:34.730
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring that deathbell for

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:38.530
notifications and I’ll see you in the next
one.

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:40.580
Assuming we both survive.

